  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nToday - Friday: Windy and Warm\n\nA low located in far northern Minnesota is quickly moving off to the \nnortheast. The increased pressure gradient from this low weakens \nduring the early afternoon which allows for the elevated winds to \ndiminish as the afternoon progresses. Wind gusts could still get up \nclose to 30 mph during the afternoon. A break in the stronger winds \noccurs tonight as shortwave ridging moves into the area. Similar to \ntoday though, another low ejects of the Rockies and into the \nnorthern plains. The pressure gradient tightens back up and the \nwinds increase as a result. Compared to today when the winds are \nmore out of the west-southwest, Friday's winds will be out of the \nsoutheast then gradually shift more southwesterly during the day. As \nfar as how strong these winds could be, the most recent REFS has a \n40 to 70% chance for winds gusts to get over 40 mph for portions of \nnortheast Iowa and southeast Minnesota, but once the values jump up \nto 45 mph, the probabilities greatly diminish to less than 5%. With \nthe south to southwesterly flow in the low to mid levels, \ntemperatures warm up into the upper 50s to low 60s for today with \nmid 60s for Friday.\n\nSaturday - Sunday: Precipitation Chances\n\nAs this low shifts off to the northeast, a cold front moves across \nthe Upper Midwest bringing about the return of precipitation \nchances. Compared to the forecast yesterday, the trends have \nincreased for precipitation chances (75 to 95%). Rain amount \nprobabilities have also increased for at least 0.5\" and 1\" when \nlooking at both of the NBM and LREF. The current LREF probability \nfor at least 0.5\" to occur across the region is now between 50 and \n70% and the probability for at least 1\" is now 15 to 25%. There is \nalso a little MUCAPE available <150 J/kg so rumble or two of thunder \ncannot be ruled out, mainly for areas south of I-90. All \nprecipitation chances end by Sunday afternoon. With the timing of \nthe frontal passage occurring Saturday afternoon, high temperatures \nremain in the upper 50s to low 60s then behind the front, high \ntemperatures for Sunday largely stay in the low to mid 40s. \n\nNext Week: Cooler and Dry\n\nTemperatures will remain on the cool side for next week as our area \nsits under cooler Canadian air through at least midweek. \nSynoptically the region will sit under a trough for Sunday and \nMonday then a shortwave ridge for Tuesday then back to a trough for \nmidweek. Despite there being a ridge for Tuesday, temperatures will \nnot warm up as we are too far north to experience warm southwest \nflow. With all of this cold air, highs are looking to stay mostly in \nthe 30s to low 40s with low temperatures in the mid teens to low \n20s. The next chance for any precipitation would be with passage of \nthe trough on Wednesday, although current ensemble members from both \nthe GEFS and EPS favor the region staying dry during the trough \npassage.",
